-- THE ONLY IN-TREE COVERAGE OF `W-OPEN-GOAL-COMMITTED` IN A NON-ENTRY MODULE, and
-- therefore the only thing that grades the allowlist entry `runBuildWarnCodes` gained
-- for it (#3027 / D3, `compiler/driver/diagnostics.mdk`).
--
-- Why a new fixture rather than a row on an existing one: both wild members of the
-- class (`make t4-census`) are single-file programs, and single-file `run`/`build`
-- widen past `runBuildWarnCodes` entirely -- they emit the FULL warning channel. So no
-- single-file fixture can tell an allowlisted code from an unlisted one, and deleting
-- `openGoalCommitWarnCode` from the list reddened nothing until this fixture existed.
-- The multi-module arm is where the allowlist is the only thing consulted.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived, not captured). `libPick`'s goal is
-- `Sh (Q Int Bool (List a))`, `a` unbound: §6.2 T4 says a goal not closed at the point
-- `inst` is applied is DEFERRED, so `T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE`'s reject must NOT fire --
-- this program is ACCEPTED on every verb. What the implementation actually does is
-- commit to the first-declared of the two ⊑-incomparable heads (`impl Sh (Q Int y z)`,
-- value 1) -- the #1183 residue, which `W-OPEN-GOAL-COMMITTED` makes audible instead of
-- silent. Declaration order therefore decides the printed value; do not read `1` as a
-- correct answer, read it as the measurement.
--
-- The declarations are ALSO an §6.1 condition (a) overlap, so `W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS`
-- rides alongside on every verb. That is a second, already-allowlisted code at a
-- different site (the `impl`, not the goal); the section-5 rows name the open-goal code
-- by its own text so one cannot stand in for the other.
import lib.{libPick}

main = println libPick
